<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Adapted from FOPP "Introduction: Iteration". GNU FDL 1.3; see ch-loops.ptx. -->
<section xml:id="sec-intro-iteration">
  <title>Introduction: Iteration</title>

  <p>
    A basic building block of all programs is the power to repeat some code over and over.
    A bank updates the balance of millions of customers every night.
    A video game moves every character on the screen sixty times a second.
    A school sends a message to every parent on a list.
    Nobody writes that code out one line per customer, character, or parent.
    They write it once and tell the computer to repeat it.
  </p>

  <p>
    In programming, repeating code is called <term>iteration</term>.
    Each time the code runs is one <term>iteration</term>, or one <term>pass</term>.
    The tool we use to repeat code is called a <term>loop</term>.
  </p>

  <note>
    <title>The test's definition</title>
    <p>
      Operation Spark's test defines a loop this way:
      <q>In order to repeat a sequence of code a specific number of times, we can use a loop.</q>
      Learn those words for the test.
      Later you will meet loops that repeat until something happens, without a set number of times, but this is the idea to start with.
    </p>
  </note>

  <p>
    Here is a program that prints the same message five times without a loop.
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="try-loops-intro-1">
    <title>Five Lines by Hand</title>
    <program xml:id="ac-loops-intro-1" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
    <code>
console.log("Practice makes perfect");
console.log("Practice makes perfect");
console.log("Practice makes perfect");
console.log("Practice makes perfect");
console.log("Practice makes perfect");
    </code>
  </program>
  </activity>

  <p>
    That works, but think about what happens if you need it 500 times.
    Or if you need to change the message: now you have to change it in five places,
    and if you miss one, your program is wrong.
  </p>

  <p>
    Here is the same program with a loop.
    You will learn how every part of it works in the next section.
    For now, run it and see that it does the same thing.
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="try-loops-intro-2">
    <title>Five Lines with a Loop</title>
    <program xml:id="ac-loops-intro-2" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
    <code>
for (let i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) {
  console.log("Practice makes perfect");
}
    </code>
  </program>
  </activity>

  <p>
    To print the message 500 times, you change one number.
    To change the message, you change one line.
    Programmers have a name for this idea: <term>D.R.Y.</term>, which stands for
    <q>Don't Repeat Yourself.</q>
    When you see the same lines of code over and over, that is a sign you need a loop.
  </p>

  <p>
    Loops are also how we work with arrays.
    In the last chapter you used bracket notation to get one element at a time:
    <c>names[0]</c>, <c>names[1]</c>, <c>names[2]</c>.
    With a loop, you can visit every element in an array, no matter how long it is.
  </p>
</section>
